  "raw_text": "ose details, Sarah. It sounds like a convenient and solo lunch ritual. How does price change your choice? For example, if the Big Mac cost two dollars more, what would you do? User: I would order something cheaper. The Big Mac's already getting more expensive. It's at the maximum I'm willing to spend. Agent: I understand. So, if the price increased by two dollars, you'd opt for a cheaper alternative. What if there was a deal on the Big Mac? What would you do differently then? User: I'd probably buy the Big Mac more if there was a deal. Agent: That makes sense. A good deal can definitely influence how often you choose an item. Moving on, over the past two weeks, what have been your most common fast-food patterns? This includes when you eat it, what you usually order, and what typically triggers it. User: I don't eat it as much lately because we're on a budget and I'm trying not to spend money. If I get a craving, usually during the lunchtime, I'll go once every two weeks. Agent: Than",
